WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.571
Hi, I'm Nibble!

00:00:02.571 --> 00:00:07.269
Tencent's Hunyuan Hy3 stores two hundred ninety-five billion parameters, but uses

00:00:07.269 --> 00:00:08.977
twenty-one billion per token.

00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:11.113
Here's how the pieces fit.

00:00:12.392 --> 00:00:15.537
Only about twenty-one billion parameters work on each token.

00:00:15.537 --> 00:00:20.080
In every layer, a router picks eight of a hundred and ninety-two experts.

00:00:21.917 --> 00:00:23.982
A token climbs eighty decoder layers.

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:27.080
Each one is attention, then a mixture of experts.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.861
Attention uses grouped queries: sixty-four query heads share just eight key-value heads,

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:34.404
so the cache is eight times smaller.

00:00:34.824 --> 00:00:38.102
Then a router sends the token to the top eight of a

00:00:38.102 --> 00:00:39.195
hundred and ninety-two experts.

00:00:39.195 --> 00:00:41.654
Roughly seven percent of the weights do the work.

00:00:42.074 --> 00:00:46.277
One extra layer, a multi-token prediction head, drafts the next token so

00:00:46.277 --> 00:00:49.079
the main model can verify several per step.

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:54.044
At 256K tokens, that cache is about eighty gigabytes.

00:00:54.044 --> 00:00:58.416
With full multi-head attention it would be six hundred and forty.

00:00:58.416 --> 00:01:01.198
Grouping is what makes the window fit.

00:01:02.442 --> 00:01:04.389
One set of weights, one knob.

00:01:04.389 --> 00:01:07.310
No think answers directly: the default, and the cheapest.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.416
Low adds a moderate chain of thought.

00:01:10.661 --> 00:01:11.463
High thinks deeply.

00:01:11.463 --> 00:01:14.135
You pay for reasoning only when the task needs it.

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:17.597
Tencent trained a 256K window.

00:01:17.597 --> 00:01:20.033
That's the one to lean on.

00:01:20.453 --> 00:01:24.962
A community build uses YaRN to stretch positions to a million tokens.

00:01:24.962 --> 00:01:28.344
That extends reach, not competence, so measure it first.

00:01:29.892 --> 00:01:34.842
Hy3's claim is efficiency: sparse experts, a shared-key cache and a draft

00:01:34.842 --> 00:01:38.142
head make a 295B model cheap to serve.

00:01:39.942 --> 00:01:44.520
To recap: eight of a hundred ninety-two experts, a cache shrunk by

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.572
shared heads, and a trained window of 256K.

00:01:47.992 --> 00:01:50.032
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:50.032 --> 00:01:50.614
I'm Nibble.

00:01:50.614 --> 00:01:50.906
Bye!
